<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dissolution of the Soviet Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Collapse of the Soviet Union, Breakup of the Soviet Union</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was the breakup of the USSR into fifteen independent states in 1991. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>'s reforms had loosened central control, and after a failed coup by hardliners in August 1991 the republics declared independence one after another. On 8 December the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> agreed to replace the union with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>; Gorbachev resigned on 25 December, and the Soviet Union formally ceased to exist the next day.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After victory in 1945 the United States became one of two superpowers, and with the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> in 1991 it remained the only one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>It also set off a chain reaction across the Soviet Union’s satellite states, the mostly bloodless Revolutions of 1989, in Polish the Autumn of Nations, which overthrew each Moscow-imposed regime and led in the end to the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> in the early 1990s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The end of the Cold War and the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> then made capitalism a truly global system, and the neoliberal global economy would have been impossible without the fall of communism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Vladimir Lazarev's words of 1984, slower and more fragile in tone, became popular after the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> in 1991; other versions came with the constitutional crisis of 1993 and, after 1991, a White Army version by Andrei Mingalyov.</blockquote><a href="/songs/farewell-of-slavianka">Farewell of Slavianka</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b>, the Supreme Soviet of the republic reinstated it as Armenia's national anthem on 1 July 1991, with several words changed from the 1918 version.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mer-hayrenik">Mer Hayrenik (Our Fatherland)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> in 1991, Russia took the wordless &quot;Patriotic Song&quot; as its anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
